profile: tinyrustlm.next-goal.current.v1
updated_utc: 2026-09-19T17:18:30Z
canonical_memory: wiki/runtime-mirust/reports/tinyrustlm-working-product-memory-2026-09-19.md
report_decision_index: wiki/runtime-mirust/reports/tinyrustlm-report-intake-decision-index-2026-09-19.md

goal: Deliver the owner-requested current-only ecosystem cleanup and a genuinely useful TinyRustLM release. The wider goal remains incomplete; a research or memory update is not a working product.

start:
  - Read startup, workspace, totem/taboo, current canonical synthesis and decision index; historical receipts are not current instructions.
  - Inspect active intake buckets, canonical Git identity/status and affected current contracts; preserve unrelated work.
  - Keep early-alpha/no-working-release status and owner official acceptance explicit. No debt, obsolete code or legacy support until acceptance.

ranked_next_work:
  - E1 model/evaluation role: resolve a few genuinely distinct exact source identities and provenance, then run the smallest admitted reference screen for usefulness, correction/conflict/resource handling.
  - E2 runtime/numerical role: resolve material source-authority gates and create fresh current registration; compare pinned reference, reconstructed Q8, scalar and real WASM with teacher-forced IDs and bounded first-divergence traces.
  - E3 browser role: measure actual qualified-candidate cold/warm load, phase peak memory, first token, generation, cancellation and multi-turn state on declared supported devices.
  - E4 release/acquisition role: discover actual hosting capabilities read-only while model work proceeds; prepare authorized immutable MiniModel seed transfer and external integrity/resume proof after qualification.
  - E5 product/owner role: verify clean first visit -> useful conversation -> offline return -> safe update/cancel; require official owner acceptance for a fully working release.

parallel_dependencies:
  - Candidate metadata review, public development rubrics, semantic contract inspection, first-run state design and hosting discovery can proceed independently; this does not authorize extra agents.
  - Source/runtime/material tests retain their existing gates. No real v3 registration or trace follows from C30 fixture tests.
  - The pinned training lane needs independent reviews/holdout custody, correct inference/masking and fresh bindings before a bounded pilot; 200000-record scale work waits for pilot value.
  - Both MemoryEndpoints lanes and campaign milestones retain separate evidence requirements; do not use their long-term totals as a substitute for the first-release dependency graph.

continue_cleanup:
  progress: docs/ecosystem-cleanup-2026-09-12.md
  lessons: wiki/runtime-mirust/reports/ecosystem-experiment-lessons-2026-09-12.md
  publication: C31 local canonical-input refactor is tested; resolve actual remote publication/exclusion/recovery capability before deployment. Do not reintroduce source snapshots or treat local transfer mocks as remote proof.
  owner_deferral: Skip ternary/Bonsai/Hadamard research for now and continue implementation toward the working product.
  blocked: Two installer links and earlier reviewed deletion cohorts remain policy-blocked. Never retry through another API, rename, truncation or parent deletion.

stop_doing:
  - Renaming rejected weights; training around unknown inference defects; unbounded model/quantizer searches; public cases relabeled as holdout.
  - Treating GGUF/SLM3/OPFS/GPU/object storage proposals as mandatory changes or accepting invented report measurements.
  - Scattered source/model copies, obsolete executable paths, hidden answer repair, replay of closed experiments, false independent reviewers or lowered gates.

report_daily: State implemented change, actual tests/results, public deployment evidence, blockers, next decision and abandoned approaches with retained lessons separately.
release_rule: Commit/push each coherent verified change from canonical source; deploy only after the relevant complete capability, authorization and external-readback gates.
